<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="f0361e4e-a359-4f54-a200-6e8a11b94dff" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerSampleDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <para>The SchedulerService project shows a Windows Service using DemiCode Scheduler,
                reading in the configuration from a configuration file. The service uses a five-second
                interval timer in order to issue messages to the Application Event Log.</para>
    </introduction>

   
            
                
            
            
<section>
  <title>Prerequisites</title>
  <content>
    <para>Navigate to the <legacyBold>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1\Samples</legacyBold> folder. Unzip the <legacyBold>SchedulerSamples.zip</legacyBold> in the Samples folder.</para>
  </content>
</section>

                
            
            
<section>
  <title>To build the sample using the Command Prompt</title>
  <content>
    <list class="ordered">
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Open the Visual Studio 2005 Command Prompt window and navigate to the <legacyBold>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1\Samples</legacyBold> folder.</para>
                    </listItem>
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Type <legacyBold>msbuild SchedulerService.sln</legacyBold> at the command line.</para>
                    </listItem>
                </list>
  </content>
</section>

                
            
            
<section>
  <title>To build the sample using Visual Studio</title>
  <content>
    <list class="ordered">
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Open Windows Explorer and navigate to the <legacyBold>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1\Samples</legacyBold>
                            folder.</para>
                    </listItem>
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Double-click the <legacyBold>SchedulerService.sln</legacyBold> to open the file in Visual Studio.</para>
                    </listItem>
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            In the <legacyBold>Build</legacyBold> menu, select <legacyBold>Build Solution</legacyBold>.</para>
                    </listItem>
                </list>
  </content>
</section>

                
            
            
<section>
  <title>To run the sample</title>
  <content>
    <definitionTable>
                    <definition>
                        The service must be installed as a service in order to run. Follow these
                        steps:
                        <list class="ordered">
                            <listItem>Open the Visual Studio 2005 Command Prompt window and navigate to the directory that contains the new
                                executable.</listItem>
                            <listItem>Install the service using <legacyBold>InstallUtil.exe</legacyBold>. This program is part of the .NET
                                Framework and can usually be located in C:\Windows\Microsoft.Net\Framework\v2.50727.<para/>
                                The following command line will install SchedulerService:<para/>
                                <code language="other">InstallUtil SchedulerService.exe</code>
                            </listItem>
                            <listItem>Locate the <legacyBold>Services</legacyBold> application (Control Panel->Administrative Tools->Services) and select the service called <legacyBold>DemiCode Scheduler Service sample</legacyBold>. Click Start in the toolbar.</listItem>
                            <listItem>Locate the <legacyBold>Event Viewer</legacyBold> application (Control Panel->Administrative Tools->Event Viewer)
                                and select the <legacyBold>Application event log</legacyBold>.</listItem>
                                <listItem>
                                Refresh the event log using the F5 key. You should now see messages from SchedulerService appearing.
                                </listItem>
                        </list>
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        After trying out the Scheduler service, follow these steps to uninstall the service:
                        <list class="ordered">
                            <listItem>Again, locate the <legacyBold>Services</legacyBold> application and select the <legacyBold>DemiCode Scheduler Service sample</legacyBold> service. Click Stop in the toolbar. </listItem>
                            <listItem>Uninstall the service using InstallUtil.exe.<para/>
                                The following command line will uninstall SchedulerService:<para/>
                                <code language="other">
                                InstallUtil /u SchedulerService.exe</code>
                            </listItem>
                        </list>
                    </definition>
                </definitionTable>
  </content>
</section>

                
            
            
<section>
  <title>Requirements</title>
  <content>
    <para>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1 and .NET Framework 2.0.</para>
  </content>
</section>

            
            
<section>
  <title>Remarks</title>
  <content>
    <definitionTable>
                    <definition>
                        This sample demonstrates the use of the .NET configuration system to configure our
                        scheduler. Also the ability to hook event handlers to the scheduler, either at the
                        top level (recieving events from all schedules) or at a specific schedule.
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        We can also see how easy it is to establish a scheduling Windows Service with just
                        a few lines of code.
                        <code language="c#">
protected override void OnStart(string[] args)
{
    // 1. Create default scheduler from configuration
    _scheduler = SchedulerFactory.CreateFromConfig("scheduling");

    // 2. Hook up to events
    _scheduler.ScheduledAlarm += new EventHandler&lt;AlarmEventArgs&gt;(_scheduler_ScheduledAlarm);
    _scheduler[&quot;FiveSecInterval&quot;].Alarm += new EventHandler&lt;AlarmEventArgs&gt;(fiveSecInterval_Alarm);

    // 3. Start the engine
    _scheduler.Start();
}
                        </code>
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        One thing to pay attention to is how we <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false" autoUpgrade="true">M:DemiCode.Scheduling.Scheduler.Dispose</codeEntityReference>
                        our scheduler instance. This is important since we're holding on to a valuable timer
                        resource.
                        <code language="c#">
protected override void OnStop()
{
    if (_scheduler != null)
    {
        _scheduler.Dispose();
        _scheduler = null;
    }
}
                    </code>
                        Since, in a Windows service, we have limited control over when the service will
                        be stopped you should consider overriding the <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="true" autoUpgrade="true">M:System.ServiceProcess.ServiceBase.OnShutdown</codeEntityReference>
                        and the <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="true" autoUpgrade="true">M:System.ServiceProcess.ServiceBase.OnPowerEvent(System.ServiceProcess.PowerBroadcastStatus)</codeEntityReference> to handle more critical events.
                        
                    </definition>   
                </definitionTable>
  </content>
</section>

        
    

